---
import Project from "../components/project.astro";
import "../css.css";

const falafelAlt = "a 3d render of a piece of falafel spinning around, orbited by a ring of sesame seeds and other ambiguous falafel wrap ingredients";
const mythAlt = "a photograph of a photobook, open to a spread with two images. The left image depicts some colorful stairs, and the right image depicts a colorful outdoor swimming pool";
---

<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<meta charset="utf-8" />
		<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
		<meta name="generator" content={Astro.generator} />
		<title>DRP4ITP</title>
	</head>
	<body>
		<header>
            <h1 aria-label="Duncan Petrie is applying to the Interactive Telecommunications Program" tabindex="0">
                <span>
                    <span>D</span>
                    <span style="--chars: 5.3; --color: green;">uncan</span>
                    <span>P</span>
                    <span style="--chars: 18; --color: red;">etrie is applying to the</span>
                </span>
                <span aria-hidden="true">
                    →
                </span>
                <span>
                    <span>I</span>
                    <span style="--chars: 8.5; --color: cadetblue;">nteractive</span>
                    <span>T</span>
                    <span style="--chars: 16; margin-left: -0.125em; --color: orange;">elecommunications</span>
                    <span>P</span>
                    <span style="--chars: 6; --color: blue;">rogram</span>
                </span>
            </h1>
		</header>
		<main>
			<Project title="Spiders on the Web" year="2026" tags={["speaking", "website"]} preview="arachnophobia.jpg">
                <p class="half">
                    In late 2026 I spoke at <a href="https://webdevconf.com/events/2026/" target="_blank" title="Web Dev Conf 2026">Web Dev Conf</a> and <a href="https://londonwebstandards.org/talks/bringing-spiders-to-the-web-with-requestanimationframe/" target="_blank" title="">London Web Standards</a> about building whimsical web animations from scratch. We learned about frame-based animation, faberge eggs, and spring physics.
                </p>
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/talk.webp" alt="duncan giving a talk at web dev conf in bristol, october 2026" />
                    <figcaption>Live demo 😬 at WDC.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p class="half">
                    The talk culminated in <a href="https://spiders.duncanpetrie.com" target="_blank" title="https://spiders.duncanpetrie.com">spiders.duncanpetrie.com</a>, a little playground where you can be a spider. The site is multiplayer, so bring your friends! If you get close enough together, you’ll start kissing (this is not a joke).
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    I also built a <a href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/a-spider-on-the-web/jnlhooedjlgccjkicbbolgbojlaigkle?authuser=0&hl=en" target="_blank" title="a spider on the web - chrome web store">browser extension</a>, which lets you take your spider on the go. It also periodically makes spiders run across your screen. This is a fun addition to the web browsing experience, and does not activate your fight-or-flight instincts.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    I really enjoyed putting this together. 
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    See <a href="https://wdc.duncanpetrie.com" target="_blank" title="https://wdc.duncanpetrie.com">wdc.duncanpetrie.com</a> for slides and more details. A recording is available on <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=88_qqxNvZmQ" target="_blank" title="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=88_qqxNvZmQ">YouTube</a>.
                </p>
                <br/>
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/arachnophobia.jpg" alt="spiders on the wikipedia page for arachnophobia" />
                    <figcaption>Spiders crawling all over the Wikipedia page for ‘arachnophobia’.</figcaption>
                </figure>
			</Project>
			<Project title="Planet Falafel" year="2026" tags={["website", "writing"]} preview="planet-falafel.webp">
                <figure class="hal">
                    <img src="/images/falafel.png" alt="screenshot of the landing page of falafel.fyi" draggable="false" />
                </figure>
				<p class="half">
					<a href="https://falafel.fyi" target="_blank" title="falafel.fyi">https://falafel.fyi</a> is a microblog about my favorite kind of ball. Every time I order a falafel wrap from a new cart, cafe, or restaurant, I write a little review, and put it there.
				</p>
				<p class="half">
					It also has an <a href="https://falafel.fyi/about" target="_blank" title="falafel.fyi/about">essay about the origins of falafel</a>, in case you’re hungry for history.
				</p>
			</Project>
            <Project title="Pedigree" year="2026" tags="writing" preview="dog-pool.jpg">
                <blockquote class="half">
                    Johnny was always a bad husband, but I didn’t know it at first. I’d never hung off someone’s arm like that. Strangers gave me flowers, and I had to wear sunglasses indoors, because the paparazzi flashes gave me headaches.
                </blockquote>
                <p class="half">
                    In August, I won a <a href="https://www.quarter--mile.com/1picture1000words/original" title="https://www.quarter--mile.com/1picture1000words" target="_blank">short story competition</a> with a story about dog adultery. The prompt was to write exactly 1000 words about this picture:
                </p>
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/dog-pool.jpg" alt="a huge fawn mastiff sits upright, like a person, on a dark wicker patio sofa, looking out at a turquoise swimming pool" draggable="false" />
                    <figcaption>
                        Is a picture truly worth a thousand words? Is <em>this</em> picture worth a thousand words?
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p class="half">
                    You can read the full story here: <a href="https://www.quarter--mile.com/1picture1000words/winners/pedigree" target="_blank" title="pedigree - 1 picture 1000 words">https://www.quarter--mile.com/1picture1000words/winners/pedigree</a>.
                </p>
            </Project>
            <Project title="Sketchbooks" year="2026 → ?" tags="drawing" preview="ds.webp">
                <p class="half">
                    I have a daily drawing practice. Mostly, I use a fine-liner and draw in tiny boxes. I’m a perfectionist, and drawing small makes it easier for me to experiment; if I make a mistake, there’s a lot of space to try again!
                </p>
                <figure>
                    <div class="image-row">
                        <img src="/images/d1.webp" alt="little drawings in a little notebook" />
                        <img src="/images/d2.webp" alt="little drawings in a little notebook" />
                        <img src="/images/d3.webp" alt="little drawings in a little notebook" />
                        <img src="/images/d4.webp" alt="little drawings in a little notebook" />
                        <img src="/images/d5.webp" alt="little drawings in a little notebook" />
                        <img src="/images/d6.webp" alt="little drawings in a little notebook" />
                        <img src="/images/d7.webp" alt="little drawings in a little notebook" />
                        <img src="/images/d8.webp" alt="little drawings in a little notebook" />
                    </div>
                    <figcaption>Some tiny drawings in a tiny notebook.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p class="half">
                    I also draw on a tablet! This is not as nice as drawing on paper, but very useful for making website assets. Some examples are the scroll tracker for my <a href="https://falafel.fyi/about" target="_blank" title="falafel.fyi/about">essay about falafel</a> and the <a href="https://wdc.duncanpetrie.com/playground/spring-chicken/" target="_blank" title="wdc.duncanpetrie.com/playground/spring-chicken">spring chicken demo</a> for my recent talk.
                </p>
                <p class="half">Some digital doodles:</p>
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/ipad.webp" alt="animated drawings done on a tablet" />
                    <figcaption>A collection of barely-animated drawings done done on a tablet.</figcaption>
                </figure>
            </Project>
            <Project title="To Pigeon-Fill the Sky" year="2025" tags={["photobook", "website"]} preview="pigeon-glitch.webp">
                <p class="half">
                    To Pigeon-Fill the Sky is a photographic storybook about AI, the creative process, and, of course, pigeons.
                </p>
                <p>
                    See more at <a href="https://pigeons.duncanpetrie.com" target="_blank" title="pigeons.duncanpetrie.com">pigeons.duncanpetrie.com</a>.
                </p>
            </Project>
            <Project title="Wikiπdia" year="2025" tags={["website", "wikipedia"]} preview="wikipi.webp">
                <p class="half">
                    How do you fit one infinity into another? <a href="https://wikipi.duncanpetrie.com/" target="_blank" title="https://wikipi.duncanpetrie.com">Wikiπdia</a> is a very simple website that uses π as an index for Wikipedia articles with numbers in their titles. It’s mostly asteroids.
                </p>
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/wikipi.webp" alt="screen recording of wikiπdia, where the digits of π link to wikipedia articles with numbers in their titles" draggable="false" />
                    <figcaption>
                        The digits of π link to wikipedia articles with those numbers in their titles.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </Project>
            <Project title="Palimpsest" year="2023 → ?" tags="photobook" preview="">

            </Project>
			<Project title="Lingermyth" year="2022" tags="photobook" preview="myth-spread.webp">
				<p class="half">
                    My final degree project, Lingermyth, is a photobook book of 50 images. Photos from this project were featured in the a-n degree show guide, AOP student awards, EZIO color awards, etc etc etc
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    This project was inspired by  <em>On Yearning</em>, my undergrad dissertation (see below). Maybe you can tell, but I was also watching a lot of Wes Anderson films while making this.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    You can see the full pdf at <a href="https://duncanpetrie.com/myth.pdf" target="_blank" title="duncanpetrie.com/myth.pdf">duncanpetrie.com/myth.pdf</a>.
                </p>
			</Project>
            <Project title="On Yearning" year="2021" tags="writing" preview="diss-pages.webp">
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/yearn.jpg" alt="photograph of a gull flying across the lonely sky" draggable="false" />
                </figure>
                <blockquote class="half">
                    I was suddenly struck by profound desire. A desire for what, I was not sure. A desire to be over there, perhaps, although I could not have said where “over there” was. I was on the edge of the world, or so it felt, and I wanted to see beyond. It was a sort of nostalgia, but not for any real past. A nostalgia for the future, maybe, the future that can no longer be.
                </blockquote>
                <p class="half">
                    I wrote my undergraduate dissertation, <em>On Yearning,</em> about the feeling I get when I look to the horizon and long to see beyond it.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    It describes art as a form of worldbuilding, and it’s the foundation of my photographic practice.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    It is available here: <a href="https://duncanpetrie.com/yearning.pdf" target="_blank" title="on yearning">duncanpetrie.com/yearning.pdf</a>.
                </p>
            </Project>
            <Project title="The Commons" year="2021" tags={["film", "wikipedia"]} preview="wiki.gif">
                <p class="half">
                    The full breadth of humanity, every hope and every sorrow, every song and every sigh, is on <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Main_Page" target="_blank" title="commons.wikimedia.org">Wikimedia Commons</a>, an open, public file repository maintained by the Wikimedia Foundation.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    In Spring of 2021, during a Covid lockdown, I created this film (<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JRXZAaDxGCQ/" target="_blank" title="youtube: a triumph of the commons">watch on youtube</a>) using 144 of those files.
                </p>
                <iframe class="half" style="aspect-ratio: 16/9; margin-block-end: 4rem;" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/JRXZAaDxGCQ?si=Y26oNIntLkXSUN8b" title="YouTube video player" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen></iframe>
            </Project>
		</main>
        <footer>
            <p class="half">
                Thank you for your consideration!
            </p>
            <p class="half">
                I made this website by hand on a rainy afternoon in November. It’s a very simple <a href="https://astro.build" target="_blank" title="astro.build">astro</a> project; my goal was to build a nice website without any client-side javascript, in case you’re reviewing applications on netscape navigator, your smart fridge, or a braille display.
            </p>
            <p class="half">
                Text on this page is set in <a href="https://samporritt.com/vevey-positive" target="_blank" title="samporritt.com/vevey-positive">vevey</a> and <a href="https://github.com/namesakefyi/atkinson-hyperlegible-soft" target="_blank" title="github.com/namesakefyi/atkinson-hyperlegible-soft">atkinson hyperlegible soft</a>.
            </p>
            <p class="half">
                Find me on <a href="https://instagram.com/probablyduncan" target="_blank" title="https://instagram.com/probablyduncan">instagram</a>, <a href="https://are.na/puncan-detrie" target="_blank" title="https://are.na/puncan-detrie">arena</a>, <a href="https://github.com/probablyduncan" target="_blank" title="https://github.com/probablyduncan">github</a>, or <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/probablyduncan/" target="_blank" title="https://www.linkedin.com/in/probablyduncan/">linkedin</a>.
            </p>
        </footer>
	</body>
</html>
<style>
	body {
		width: min(100vw, 100vh);
		width: min(100vw, 64rem);
		margin: 0 auto;
		padding: 0 0.25em;
	}

	header {
        height: 50svh;
        
        h1 {
            display: grid;
            font-family: var(--vevey);
            font-size: 1.618em;
            grid-template-columns: repeat(3, auto);
            padding-bottom: 0.25em;

            margin: 0;
            position: sticky;
            top: 0.125em;
        }

        h1:focus-visible {
            outline: none;
            /* color: blue; */
        }

        h1 > :nth-child(2) {
            text-align: left;
        }

        h1 > :last-child {
            text-align: right;
        }

        * {
            color: var(--color);
        }

        h1 > span > span:nth-child(even) {
            display: inline-block;
            white-space: pre;
            transform-origin: 0%;
            scale: 0 1;
            width: 0;
            transition: scale 0.2s ease-out, width 0.2s ease-out;
        }

        h1:is(:hover, :focus-visible) > span > span:nth-child(even) {
            --scale: 0.618;
            scale: var(--scale) 1;
            width: calc(var(--chars) * var(--scale) * 1ch);
        }
	}

    .half {
        @media (min-width: 540px) {
            width: 87.5%;
        }
        @media (min-width: 860px) {
            width: 50%;
        }
    }

    p {
		text-align: justify;
    }

    blockquote {
        font-style: italic;
        margin-block: 1.5em;
        margin-right: -1rem;
        margin-left: 0.5rem;
        padding-left: 1rem;
        border-left: 0.125em dashed alpha(from currentColor / 20%);
        /* color: alpha(from currentColor / 60%); */
        text-wrap: balance;

        em {
            
        }
    }

	p, figcaption {
        padding-inline-start: 0.5rem;
        @media (max-width: 721px) {
            padding-inline-end: 0.5rem;
        }
	}

    a {
        color: red;
        &:visited {
            color: indianred
        }
        &:hover {
            text-decoration-style: wavy;
            text-decoration-skip-ink: none;
        }
    }

    figure {
        margin-inline: 0;
    }

    figcaption {
        color: alpha(from currentColor / 0.5);
        font-size: 0.875em;
        width: 87.5%;
        text-wrap: balance;
    }

    footer {
        margin-block: 4rem;
    }

    .image-row {
        display: flex;
        width: 100%;
        overflow-x: scroll;
        gap: 1rem;
        margin-bottom: 0.1rem;

        img {
            max-width: 40%;
        }
    }
</style>